00:07:06.361 --> 00:07:10.321
A no-trade theorem is a result about information, and that is how it works.

00:07:10.321 --> 00:07:15.741
Shared starting beliefs, plus common knowledge that everyone is rational, make a private

00:07:15.741 --> 00:07:17.341
signal a week reason to bet.

00:07:17.921 --> 00:07:22.941
The offer is itself news, fold the other side's willingness to take the opposite side back

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:26.601
into your estimate, and the gap that made the trade look good shrinks.

00:07:27.241 --> 00:07:31.461
Pass that message enough times and a purely informational disagreement can vanish.

00:07:32.061 --> 00:07:34.261
What remains is a refusal, not a price.

00:07:35.001 --> 00:07:37.861
Picture a security that pays according to a state of the world.

00:07:37.861 --> 00:07:39.781
Both traders share a prior.

00:07:40.501 --> 00:07:41.961
One of them sees something private.

00:07:42.481 --> 00:07:47.001
If rationality is common knowledge, the first bid tells the second something about that

00:07:47.001 --> 00:07:49.861
signal, and the answer tells the first something back.

00:07:50.481 --> 00:07:51.621
Each round is a message.

00:07:52.261 --> 00:07:57.601
The classic claim, as a new computational paper restates it, attributes trade to heterogeneous

00:07:57.601 --> 00:07:58.261
beliefs.

00:07:58.881 --> 00:08:01.441
People bet because they did not start in the same place.

00:08:02.081 --> 00:08:06.861
Under a shared prior, information alone is not supposed to clear a mutually acceptable bet.

00:08:06.861 --> 00:08:13.321
Will AI trade? A computational inversion of the no-trade theorem keeps those common beliefs and

00:08:13.321 --> 00:08:18.861
asks whether limited computation can start the betting again. Agents play an unfolding game.

00:08:19.481 --> 00:08:25.741
Power sets how complex a strategy they can run. The finding flips the textbook. A stable no-trade

00:08:25.741 --> 00:08:31.361
Nash equilibrium shows up only when almost rational agents have slightly different computational power.

00:08:31.361 --> 00:08:36.821
give them identical power and they may never converge. The adjustments they keep making are,

00:08:37.041 --> 00:08:42.821
the authors say, a kind of trade. Let them strategically underuse their compute and the instability

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:49.021
worsens. In matching pennies, that choice wipes out any chance of equilibrium. A busy screen

00:08:49.021 --> 00:08:54.461
on this account need not mean someone is simply wrong. It can mean two equally powerful

00:08:54.461 --> 00:09:00.441
reasoners cannot finish the regressive I know that you know. No trade under verifiable information

